            "text": "Slides a short pattern along an array and, at each position, multiplies the overlapping values together and adds them up. The sum is large where the array looks like the pattern, so the peaks of the output mark where the pattern occurs.\n\nUse it to find a known shape in a recording - a gesture, a beat, a pulse - or, with two signals, to measure how far one lags behind the other: correlate one against the other and the peak's distance from the centre is the lag.\n\nIt is np.convolve without the kernel reversed. For a symmetric pattern (1 2 1) the two are the same; for 1 2 3 they differ, and np.correlate is the one that matches the pattern as written.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.correlate <pattern: floats ...> <mode>\nDefaults: no pattern (send one to the pattern inlet), mode same.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.correlate 1 2 3\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array to search (a list is turned into one). Every input is correlated and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\npattern:\nWhat to look for, replacing any given as arguments. Held: send it first.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nFor a 2-D or larger array, the axis to slide along: -1 (the default) along each row, 0 down each column.\n\nmode:\nAn option, under the node: same (the default, output the length of the input), full (every overlap) or valid (only where the pattern lies wholly inside), as for np.convolve.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ncorrelated:\nThe match score at each position.\n\nHEIGHT IS NOT ONLY LIKENESS:\nThe score grows with the size of the values as well as with how alike they are, so a loud passage scores higher than a quiet one that matches better. To compare shape alone, subtract the mean and divide by the size of both first.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n1 2 3 appears twice in the array; the correlation peaks at 14 - 1x1 + 2x2 + 3x3 - over the centre of each copy, positions 3 and 9. np.argmax picks the first, best match. A metro re-sends the array five times a second; move the 1 2 3 and click.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.convolve is the same sliding sum with the kernel reversed: a filter. t.corrcoef and t.cosine_similarity compare two whole arrays at once. np.argmax finds the peak."
        },
